4872039", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Fíngen mac Áedo Duib was a King of Munster from the Eóganacht Chaisil branch of the Eoganachta. He was the great-grandson of Feidlimid mac Óengusa, a previous king. He succeeded Amalgaid mac Éndai as king in 601.", "newValue": "Fíngen mac Áedo Duib, King of Munster from 601 CE until his death in 618 CE, was a pivotal figure in the early consolidation of Eóganacht power in southern Ireland during the nascent stages of the early medieval period. As a scion of the Eóganacht Chaisil, the preeminent branch of the Eoganachta, his reign represented a crucial step in establishing the dynastic lineage that would dominate Munster for centuries. His rule occurred amidst a complex political landscape where various Eóganacht septs vied for supremacy, making his 17-year tenure a period of significant internal stabilization and external assertion for his branch.

During his kingship, Fíngen successfully navigated the intricate web of alliances and rivalries that characterized early Irish politics. He is recorded as having succeeded Amalgaid mac Éndai, a member of the rival Eóganacht Áine branch, in 601 CE, thereby re-establishing the Eóganacht Chaisil's claim to the Munster throne. His lineage, tracing back to Feidlimid mac Óengusa, a prominent earlier king, provided a strong basis for his legitimacy, which he reinforced through effective governance and strategic actions, though specific military campaigns or legislative acts are largely unrecorded due to the scarcity of contemporary sources. The period saw the gradual strengthening of the Eóganacht federation, with Cashel emerging as its spiritual and political center.

